{"id":"n-20261011-3sqp5q","category":"business","display_title":"AI Impact on Retail Prices","display_title_he":"השפעת הבינה המלאכותית על מחירי הקמעונאות","short_summary":"Retailers worldwide are adopting artificial intelligence tools, raising concerns among experts about dynamic and personalized pricing.","english_level":"C1","words":530,"reading_time":4,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3a/PIIGS_Mk_4_map.png/960px-PIIGS_Mk_4_map.png?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"The original uploader was Snow storm in Eastern Asia at Engl · CC BY 3.0","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:PIIGS_Mk_4_map.png"},"sources":[{"name":"CNBC","domain":"cnbc.com","url":"https://www.cnbc.com/2026/10/11/ai-dynamic-pricing-shoppers-groceries.html","title":"AI may change the price of your Big Mac and groceries — what this means for shoppers","published_at":"2026-10-11T05:00:01.000Z"}],"published_at":"2026-10-11T05:00:01.000Z","first_published_in_app_at":"2026-10-11T06:06:27.826Z","quality_score":9,"interest_score":9,"verification_status":"verified","content_status":"published","paragraphs":["Fast-food giants and major supermarkets are increasingly adopting artificial intelligence tools that could significantly alter the prices consumers pay for everyday goods. According to recent reports, experts warn that the proliferation of data-driven technologies might make personalized pricing far easier for retailers to deploy globally. In the United States, a federal antitrust lawsuit filed against McDonald's alleges that the fast-food corporation utilizes an AI-powered pricing engine across its locations to set menu prices and potentially overcharge patrons for items like Big Macs and french fries. In response, McDonald's has denied employing artificial intelligence to determine what individual customers are willing to pay, asserting instead that it merely supplies its franchisees with helpful resources, research, and general recommendations to guide their independent business decisions.","Meanwhile, food businesses are digitizing their daily operations in various other ways. American grocery chain Kroger announced it implements an AI platform known as FlashFood to discount perishable goods nearing expiration and market them through a dedicated mobile application. Additionally, electronic shelf labels, which present item prices on digital screens, are growing increasingly common at major supermarkets such as Kroger, Amazon Fresh, Walmart, and Whole Foods, as well as British chains like Tesco, Morrisons, and Asda. In another technological trial, financial platform Revolut tested facial recognition checkout systems in select coffee shops to allow seamless payments. Experts note that these tools significantly expand the amount of data companies gather, ranging from customer transaction histories to browsing behavior, purchasing patterns, and location.","As the normalization of retail automation expands, commercial law experts caution that it could foster more dynamic pricing. This practice involves frequent, rapid, and real-time adjustments based on fluctuating market conditions, including consumer demand, timing, store capacity, and rival prices. Miroslava Marinova, a senior lecturer at the University of East London, noted that while airlines, hotels, and ride-hailing services have used it for years, modern retail is blurring the boundary between dynamic and personalized pricing by combining broad market information with granular consumer data. Economists at the Bank of England, Clare Lombardelli and Rupal Patel, observed that advanced technology allows firms to approach what they term perfect price discrimination, charging as close as possible to the absolute maximum a consumer is willing to pay. Furthermore, such continuous shifts could complicate the work of statisticians who interpret monthly inflation data based on a representative sample.","To address these shifting market dynamics, several U.S. states are introducing legislative restrictions. New York now mandates that businesses utilizing personal data for pricing must clearly disclose the practice, while Maryland restricts food retailers from using personalized data to hike food delivery or purchase prices. Similarly, New Jersey and Connecticut have advanced measures targeting surveillance pricing to protect shoppers.","While retail analysts suggest that dynamic pricing can occasionally offer helpful discounts, making certain items more accessible, experts emphasize significant potential drawbacks. When consumers lose the ability to easily compare prices, understand why a specific rate was offered, or recognize if a quote reflects general market conditions, traditional market competition can weaken. The Bank of England economists added that this creates a splintered shopping experience where households face increasingly divergent inflation rates, meaning aggregate economic measures may no longer reflect individual experiences."],"facts":[{"fact":"Fast-food giants and supermarkets are deploying artificial intelligence tools that could influence consumer prices and enable more personalized pricing.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"A federal antitrust lawsuit against McDonald's claims the company uses an AI pricing engine across U.S. locations to overcharge customers.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"McDonald's denied using AI to determine individual customer willingness to pay, stating it provides franchisees with tools and recommendations.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Kroger utilizes an AI platform called FlashFood to mark down perishables near the end of their shelf life.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Electronic shelf labels are gaining popularity at supermarkets like Kroger, Amazon Fresh, Walmart, Whole Foods, Tesco, Morrisons, and Asda.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Revolut trialed facial recognition checkout in select coffee shops.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Experts warn that AI integration could lead to dynamic pricing, where rapid, real-time price changes occur based on demand, timing, or competitors.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"Bank of England economists Clare Lombardelli and Rupal Patel noted that sophisticated technology enables 'perfect price discrimination' by charging close to the maximum a consumer is willing to pay.","sources":[1]},{"fact":"U.S. states including New York, Maryland, New Jersey, and Connecticut are enacting measures to restrict or regulate data-driven and surveillance pricing.","sources":[1]}],"glossary":[{"text":"Fast-food","lemma":"fast-food","pos":"adj","he":"מזון מהיר","name":false},{"text":"giants","lemma":"giant","pos":"noun","he":"ענקיות","name":false},{"text":"major","lemma":"major","pos":"adj","he":"ראשיים","name":false},{"text":"supermarkets","lemma":"supermarket","pos":"noun","he":"סופרמרקטים","name":false},{"text":"increasingly","lemma":"increasingly","pos":"adv","he":"יותר ויותר","name":false},{"text":"adopting","lemma":"adopt","pos":"verb","he":"מאמצים","name":false},{"text":"artificial","lemma":"artificial","pos":"adj","he":"מלאכותי","name":false},{"text":"intelligence","lemma":"intelligence","pos":"noun","he":"בינה","name":false},{"text":"tools","lemma":"tool","pos":"noun","he":"כלים","name":false},{"text":"significantly","lemma":"significantly","pos":"adv","he":"משמעותית","name":false},{"text":"alter","lemma":"alter","pos":"verb","he":"לשנות","name":false},{"text":"prices","lemma":"price","pos":"noun","he":"מחירים","name":false},{"text":"consumers","lemma":"consumer","pos":"noun","he":"צרכנים","name":false},{"text":"pay","lemma":"pay","pos":"verb","he":"משלמים","name":false},{"text":"everyday","lemma":"everyday","pos":"adj","he":"יומיומיים","name":false},{"text":"goods","lemma":"good","pos":"noun","he":"מוצרים","name":false},{"text":"According","lemma":"accord","pos":"prep","he":"לפי","name":false},{"text":"recent","lemma":"recent","pos":"adj","he":"אחרונים","name":false},{"text":"reports","lemma":"report","pos":"